[8054,{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"result":"Bicycles stay upright mainly because a spinning wheel resists being tilted. Each wheel acts like a gyroscope: its angular momentum points along the axle, and as the bike leans, the wheel's spin tends to keep its plane steady rather than falling over with the frame. This effect is real but small. Studies have shown that a bicycle with its wheels counter-rotating still stays stable, so gyroscopes are not the whole story.\n\nThe more important mechanism is steering. When a rider begins to lean to one side, the front wheel can be turned toward that side. The contact patch of the tire then rolls in a curve, and the bike's weight shifts so that gravity and the ground's reaction force produce a torque that swings the frame back upright. Steering into the fall is what keeps the bike balanced, and it happens almost continuously and without conscious thought.\n\nThe geometry helps too. The front fork is angled so that the point where the steering axis meets the ground sits ahead of the contact patch. This offset, called trail, makes the front wheel naturally swing in the direction of a lean, much like a shopping cart wheel that trails behind its pivot. Riders still need to make small corrections, but trail provides a self-centering tendency that keeps the bike stable at speed.","session_id":"sess_fixture","total_cost_usd":0.00198562,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7799,"cache_read_input_tokens":22262,"output_tokens":406,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7799,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":406,"cache_read_input_tokens":22262,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7799,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7799},"type":"message"}],"speed